    INSERT INTO ielts_tests (slug,name,is_published,description) VALUES ('gri-reading-1','Gri Reading 1',true,'IELTS Academic Reading — Breathing Room: The Making of the Urban Public Park') RETURNING id INTO v_test;
    INSERT INTO ielts_sections (test_id,section,duration_minutes) VALUES (v_test,'reading',20) RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
    INSERT INTO ielts_parts (section_id,part_number,title,passage_html) VALUES (v_sec,1,'Breathing Room: The Making of the Urban Public Park','<p>Before the nineteenth century, open green land in and around cities was rarely designed for the general population. Aristocratic estates and royal hunting grounds offered manicured lawns and ornamental gardens, but access was restricted to the privileged few. The commons that ordinary townspeople had once used for grazing and recreation were steadily fenced off during centuries of enclosure. As industrial cities swelled with workers drawn from the countryside, the shortage of accessible outdoor space became acute. Districts grew dense and smoky, and for most labouring families a patch of grass was an unfamiliar luxury.</p><p>The turning point came in Britain during the 1830s and 1840s. Reformers, physicians and members of parliament began to argue that fresh air and exercise were not indulgences but necessities of public health. A parliamentary committee reported in 1833 that towns urgently needed open spaces where inhabitants could walk and breathe freely. The prevailing medical theory of the day held that disease spread through foul air, or miasma, rising from stagnant water and rotting matter. Parks, it was believed, would dilute this poisonous atmosphere and act as the lungs of the crowded city.</p><p>Birkenhead Park, opened near Liverpool in 1847, is often regarded as the first publicly funded park created expressly for ordinary citizens. Designed by Joseph Paxton, it was paid for out of public money and was free to enter, a deliberate contrast with the private pleasure gardens that charged admission. Its winding paths, artificial lakes and gently rolling meadows were arranged to appear entirely natural, concealing the enormous labour that had shaped them. The design proved enormously influential. When the American landscape architect Frederick Law Olmsted visited in 1850, he admired the way a democratic space could be enjoyed equally by rich and poor, and he carried those ideas home.</p><p>Olmsted, together with Calvert Vaux, went on to design New York''s Central Park, whose construction began in 1857. Their winning proposal, nicknamed the Greensward Plan, transformed a stretch of rocky, swampy ground into a vast landscape of lawns, woodlands and water. Olmsted insisted that the park should offer a restorative escape from the mental strain of commercial life, a place where the mind might be soothed by scenery. He was also a shrewd social observer, noting that a shared park could bring citizens of different classes into peaceful contact. Yet the creation of the park was not without cost: an established community of several hundred residents, including a settlement known as Seneca Village, was displaced to make way for it.</p><p>As the century advanced, the park became a standard feature of the modern city across Europe and North America. Municipal authorities laid out bandstands, boating lakes, glasshouses and formal flower beds. Access to nature was increasingly seen as a civic right rather than a private privilege, and by the early twentieth century few large cities would have considered themselves complete without one. Parks were also intended to civilise behaviour: promoters hoped that exposure to orderly greenery would encourage sobriety and self-improvement among the working classes, drawing them away from taverns and gin palaces.</p><p>The benefits attributed to parks have shifted with changing knowledge. The miasma theory that first justified them was later discredited, yet the parks it produced proved valuable for reasons their founders had not anticipated. Modern research links access to green space with lower rates of stress, improved concentration and greater physical activity. Trees filter pollutants and moderate the urban heat that accumulates among concrete and asphalt. What began as a response to a mistaken idea about disease endured because it answered deeper and more durable human needs. Today, as cities grow denser once more, planners again debate how to guarantee that every resident lives within reach of a green and open space.</p>') RETURNING id INTO v_part;
