    INSERT INTO ielts_tests (slug,name,is_published,description) VALUES ('gri-reading-5','Gri Reading 5',true,'IELTS Academic Reading — The Two Minds Behind Every Choice') RETURNING id INTO v_test;
    INSERT INTO ielts_sections (test_id,section,duration_minutes) VALUES (v_test,'reading',20) RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
    INSERT INTO ielts_parts (section_id,part_number,title,passage_html) VALUES (v_sec,1,'The Two Minds Behind Every Choice','<p>Every day, people make thousands of decisions, from the trivial to the momentous, yet remarkably few of these choices are the product of careful reasoning. According to a framework popularised by the psychologist Daniel Kahneman, the human mind relies on two distinct modes of thought. The first, which he labelled System 1, operates automatically and rapidly, demanding little or no effort. It is the mind at work when a person recognises a familiar face or completes the phrase &lsquo;bread and ______&rsquo;. The second, System 2, is slow, deliberate and effortful; it is summoned for tasks such as multiplying large numbers or filling out a tax form. Although people tend to identify with the reflective System 2, Kahneman argued that the intuitive System 1 quietly governs the majority of everyday behaviour.</p><p>System 1 achieves its speed by relying on heuristics &mdash; mental shortcuts that substitute a simple question for a harder one. When asked to judge how common a particular event is, for instance, people often resort to the availability heuristic, estimating frequency by the ease with which examples come to mind. Because dramatic events such as plane crashes are heavily reported, they are easily recalled, and so their likelihood is routinely overestimated. Heuristics are not flaws; they are efficient adaptations that usually produce reasonable answers with minimal effort. Difficulties arise only when the shortcut is applied to a situation for which it is poorly suited, and the resulting error is termed a cognitive bias.</p><p>A second shortcut, the representativeness heuristic, judges the probability that something belongs to a category by how closely it resembles a typical member of that category. This can cause people to ignore genuine statistical information, such as how rare the category is to begin with, in favour of a superficial resemblance. Among the most studied of these biases is anchoring, the tendency to rely too heavily on the first piece of information encountered. In one classic demonstration, participants who were shown an arbitrarily high number before estimating a quantity gave systematically larger estimates than those shown a low number, even though the initial figure was plainly irrelevant.</p><p>A related phenomenon is loss aversion, the observation that the pain of losing a sum of money is felt more intensely than the pleasure of gaining the same amount. This asymmetry helps explain why many people cling to failing investments rather than accept a definite loss. Bias also shapes the way information is gathered. Confirmation bias leads individuals to seek out and give greater weight to evidence that supports their existing beliefs, while discounting anything that contradicts them. The effect is not usually the result of deliberate dishonesty; rather, it reflects the mind&rsquo;s preference for coherence over accuracy.</p><p>A further complication emerges when the number of options grows. Intuitively, more choice ought to be better, yet research suggests that an abundance of alternatives can be paralysing. In a well-known study conducted in a Californian supermarket, the psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper set up a tasting booth offering samples of jam. On some days the display held twenty-four varieties; on others it held only six. The larger display attracted more passers-by, but far fewer of them actually bought a jar: only three per cent of those who saw the extensive selection made a purchase, compared with nearly thirty per cent at the limited display. The researchers concluded that too many options can overwhelm rather than liberate, a condition sometimes called choice overload.</p><p>These findings have practical consequences. Recognising that people rarely weigh every alternative rationally, some economists and policymakers have turned to &lsquo;choice architecture&rsquo; &mdash; the deliberate design of the environments in which decisions are made. A cafeteria that places fruit at eye level, or a pension scheme that enrols employees automatically unless they opt out, gently steers behaviour without removing freedom of choice. Such interventions, popularly known as nudges, remain controversial, with critics questioning whether it is legitimate to exploit the very biases that psychology has uncovered. What is no longer disputed, however, is that the rational decision-maker of classical economic theory is, at best, an incomplete portrait of how human beings actually think.</p>') RETURNING id INTO v_part;
