    INSERT INTO ielts_tests (slug,name,is_published,description) VALUES ('gri-reading-6','Gri Reading 6',true,'IELTS Academic Reading — Riders of the Steppe: Unravelling the Origins of the Domestic Horse') RETURNING id INTO v_test;
    INSERT INTO ielts_sections (test_id,section,duration_minutes) VALUES (v_test,'reading',20) RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
    INSERT INTO ielts_parts (section_id,part_number,title,passage_html) VALUES (v_sec,1,'Riders of the Steppe: Unravelling the Origins of the Domestic Horse','<p>Few animals have shaped human history as profoundly as the horse. For thousands of years, wild horses grazed the vast grasslands, or steppe, that stretch from the plains of Hungary eastward across Central Asia to Mongolia. These open landscapes, too dry for early farming, offered little to settled communities but supported enormous herds of grazing animals. Unlike cattle, sheep and goats, which had been herded since the dawn of farming, the horse was one of the last large animals to be brought under human control, entering the story of settled civilisation comparatively late. The question of when, where and how humans first tamed it has long fascinated archaeologists, and only recently has a combination of new scientific techniques begun to supply convincing answers.</p><p>For years, the strongest candidate for the earliest domestication was the Botai culture of northern Kazakhstan, which flourished around 3500 BCE. Excavations at Botai settlements uncovered enormous quantities of horse bones, suggesting a community heavily dependent on the animal. More tellingly, chemical analysis of residues preserved in fragments of pottery revealed traces of mare''s milk, implying that the Botai people did not merely hunt horses but milked them. Soil from within the settlements contained unusually high concentrations of phosphates and nitrogen, chemical signatures typical of the dung that accumulates where animals are penned, hinting at corrals or enclosures. Some horse teeth also displayed a distinctive pattern of wear thought to be caused by a bit, the mouthpiece used to control a ridden or harnessed animal.</p><p>This apparently decisive evidence was overturned by genetics. When researchers sequenced DNA from Botai horses, they discovered that these animals were not the ancestors of today''s domestic breeds at all. Instead, the Botai horses were most closely related to the Przewalski''s horse, a stocky wild species that survives in Mongolia. The Botai experiment in horse-keeping, it seems, was a genuine but separate attempt that left no lasting descendants among modern horses. The discovery was a striking reminder that a practice can be invented, flourish for a time and then vanish without shaping the history that follows.</p><p>The true origin of the modern domestic horse was pinpointed by a large study published in 2021, which compared the genomes of hundreds of ancient horses from across Eurasia. The results indicated that all present-day domestic horses descend from a population that lived in the lower Volga-Don region of the western steppe, in what is now southern Russia, around 2200 BCE. By reading the tiny mutations that accumulate in DNA from one generation to the next, the researchers could build a family tree for the species and trace its branches back to a single source. From this homeland the lineage spread with extraordinary speed, replacing local wild horses across Europe and Asia within a few centuries.</p><p>What made these particular horses so successful? Comparison of ancient genomes revealed two genetic changes that set the new lineage apart. One affected the animals'' temperament, apparently making them calmer and more tolerant of human handling; the other strengthened the back, allowing horses to bear weight more comfortably. Together these traits produced an animal well suited to being ridden over long distances, a decisive advantage that human communities were quick to exploit. Earlier lineages, by contrast, may have been too nervous or too fragile to serve as dependable mounts.</p><p>The consequences were far-reaching. Before the horse, movement across the steppe depended on human legs and slow-moving oxen. A mounted rider could travel many times faster, herd larger flocks and cover territory that had previously been beyond reach. Slightly later, the invention of the spoked wheel allowed horses to be harnessed to light chariots, which transformed warfare across the ancient world. Horses also carried goods and ideas: the rapid spread of certain languages and technologies across Eurasia is now widely linked to mobile, horse-borne peoples. In economic terms, the animal functioned as living transport, plough-power and portable wealth all at once, and societies that mastered it gained an enduring edge over those that did not. Whole ways of life were reorganised around the animal, and a distinctive nomadic, herding culture took shape on the open grasslands. From a modest herd on the western steppe, the horse went on to reshape trade, migration and conquest for the next four thousand years.</p>') RETURNING id INTO v_part;
