    INSERT INTO ielts_tests (slug,name,is_published,description) VALUES ('gri-reading-7','Gri Reading 7',true,'IELTS Academic Reading — Farming Toward the Sky') RETURNING id INTO v_test;
    INSERT INTO ielts_sections (test_id,section,duration_minutes) VALUES (v_test,'reading',20) RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
    INSERT INTO ielts_parts (section_id,part_number,title,passage_html) VALUES (v_sec,1,'Farming Toward the Sky','<p>Vertical farming refers to the practice of cultivating crops in stacked layers or on vertically inclined surfaces, typically within enclosed, climate-controlled structures such as repurposed warehouses, shipping containers, or purpose-built towers. Unlike conventional agriculture, which spreads horizontally across vast tracts of arable land, vertical farms grow food upwards, multiplying the yield obtainable from a single footprint. The concept was popularised in the early 2000s by Dickson Despommier, a professor of environmental health, who argued that concentrating food production inside cities could ease the mounting pressure on rural farmland.</p><p>At the heart of most vertical farms lies a soilless growing method. In hydroponic systems, plant roots are bathed in a nutrient-rich water solution rather than anchored in earth; in aeroponic systems, the roots hang in air and are misted periodically with the same nutrients. Both approaches recycle water continuously, and operators claim they use as much as ninety-five per cent less water than open fields. Because the plants receive precisely measured minerals, growth is rapid and predictable, and the absence of soil removes many of the pests and diseases that plague traditional crops.</p><p>Light, however, remains the decisive variable. Deprived of sunlight, indoor crops depend on arrays of light-emitting diodes, or LEDs, tuned to the red and blue wavelengths that plants absorb most efficiently for photosynthesis. Early vertical farms struggled because lighting was prohibitively expensive, but the falling cost of LED technology over the past decade has transformed the economics. Growers can now adjust the intensity and duration of light to accelerate maturation, harvesting certain leafy greens in as little as three weeks.</p><p>The advantages extend beyond water and speed. Situated within or near urban centres, vertical farms shorten the distance between grower and consumer, trimming the transport emissions and spoilage associated with long supply chains. Production is unaffected by drought, frost, or seasonal change, allowing a steady harvest throughout the year. Land that would be unusable for conventional farming, such as a derelict factory or an underground tunnel, can be pressed into service. For densely populated cities with little surrounding farmland, such reliability is an attractive prospect.</p><p>Yet the model carries significant drawbacks, the most stubborn of which is energy. The LEDs and climate-control systems that make year-round growing possible consume vast quantities of electricity, and where that power comes from fossil fuels, the environmental gains from saved water and land may be cancelled out. Critics point out that a field of wheat receives sunlight at no cost, whereas a vertical farm must pay for every photon. This expense confines most commercial operations to high-value, quick-growing produce such as herbs, salad leaves, and microgreens, rather than the calorie-dense staples such as rice, wheat, and potatoes that feed the bulk of humanity.</p><p>The financial barriers are equally daunting. Building and equipping a vertical farm demands heavy upfront investment in racks, pumps, sensors, and lighting, and running one requires skilled technicians rather than seasonal labourers. Several heavily funded start-ups have collapsed after failing to turn a profit, a reminder that enthusiasm alone cannot guarantee commercial success. Some economists argue that the sector has been sustained more by investor optimism than by proven returns.</p><p>Supporters counter that the technology is still young and improving quickly. As renewable electricity becomes cheaper and automation reduces labour costs, they predict that vertical farming will occupy a growing niche within the wider food system. Few serious advocates claim it will replace traditional agriculture; rather, they envisage it complementing conventional farms by supplying fresh, perishable produce to cities while fields continue to grow grains and livestock feed. In this view, the two methods are partners rather than rivals.</p><p>Whether vertical farming ultimately delivers on its promise may depend less on horticulture than on the broader transition to clean energy. If electricity from renewable sources becomes abundant and inexpensive, the principal objection to the practice will weaken considerably. Until then, the glowing towers of lettuce rising inside city warehouses remain a striking, if partial, glimpse of how food might be grown in an increasingly crowded world.</p>') RETURNING id INTO v_part;
