    INSERT INTO ielts_tests (slug,name,is_published,description) VALUES ('gri-reading-8','Gri Reading 8',true,'IELTS Academic Reading — The Architecture of a Night''s Rest') RETURNING id INTO v_test;
    INSERT INTO ielts_sections (test_id,section,duration_minutes) VALUES (v_test,'reading',20) RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
    INSERT INTO ielts_parts (section_id,part_number,title,passage_html) VALUES (v_sec,1,'The Architecture of a Night''s Rest','<p>For much of human history, sleep was regarded as a passive state, a nightly pause in which the brain simply switched off and waited for morning. Modern science has overturned this assumption completely. Far from being idle, the sleeping brain is intensely active, cycling through a carefully ordered sequence of stages that repeat roughly every ninety minutes. A healthy adult typically passes through four to six of these cycles each night, and disrupting them carries measurable consequences for both body and mind.</p><p>Sleep is broadly divided into two categories: rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and non-REM sleep. Non-REM sleep itself unfolds in three stages of increasing depth. The first is a brief transitional phase, lasting only a few minutes, during which the sleeper drifts between wakefulness and unconsciousness and can be roused with little effort. The second stage occupies the largest share of the night, accounting for roughly half of total sleep time in adults. It is here that heart rate slows and body temperature drops in preparation for deeper rest. The third stage, often called slow-wave sleep, is the most restorative of all. During this period the brain produces large, slow electrical waves, and it becomes considerably harder to wake the sleeper. Slow-wave sleep is thought to be essential for physical recovery, including tissue repair and the release of growth hormone.</p><p>REM sleep, by contrast, presents a striking paradox. Although the body is effectively paralysed during this phase, preventing the sleeper from acting out dreams, the brain displays patterns of activity almost indistinguishable from those of a person who is awake. It is during REM that the most vivid dreaming occurs. The proportion of REM sleep changes dramatically across a lifetime: newborn infants may spend around half of their sleep in this state, whereas in adults the figure falls to roughly a fifth. Interestingly, the length of REM periods increases as the night progresses, so the longest and most intense episodes tend to occur in the hours before waking.</p><p>Governing this entire process is the circadian rhythm, an internal timekeeper that operates on an approximately twenty-four-hour cycle. This biological clock is regulated by a small cluster of cells in the hypothalamus known as the suprachiasmatic nucleus. The clock responds chiefly to light: when darkness falls, the brain signals the release of melatonin, a hormone that promotes drowsiness, and levels of this hormone decline again as morning light returns. Because the rhythm is so tightly linked to light exposure, the artificial illumination of modern life, particularly the blue light emitted by screens, can delay melatonin production and push the natural timing of sleep later than is healthy.</p><p>Perhaps the most compelling question of all is why we sleep in the first place. No single answer has achieved universal acceptance, but several complementary theories command strong support. One holds that sleep serves a fundamentally restorative purpose, allowing the body to repair cells and conserve energy. A more recent discovery points to a remarkable cleansing function: during sleep, the spaces between brain cells expand, allowing a wash of fluid to flush out metabolic waste products that accumulate during waking hours. Among the substances cleared is beta-amyloid, a protein associated with Alzheimer''s disease, a finding that has intensified interest in the link between poor sleep and cognitive decline.</p><p>Sleep also plays an indispensable role in memory. While we rest, the brain replays and reorganises the experiences of the day, transferring information from temporary storage into more durable long-term memory. This process, known as memory consolidation, appears to depend on both slow-wave and REM sleep working in concert. Slow-wave sleep is particularly associated with the strengthening of factual and event-based memories, whereas REM sleep seems to help integrate new knowledge with older information and may support creative problem-solving. Experiments have repeatedly shown that people who sleep after learning a new skill perform better than those who remain awake for an equivalent period.</p><p>The practical implications of this research are considerable. Chronic sleep deprivation has been linked to impaired concentration, weakened immunity, and an elevated risk of conditions ranging from obesity to heart disease. Yet in many societies short sleep is worn almost as a badge of honour, a sign of dedication and ambition. Sleep scientists argue that this attitude is dangerously misguided. Rather than a luxury to be sacrificed, sleep is emerging in the scientific literature as one of the most powerful and underappreciated pillars of human health.</p>') RETURNING id INTO v_part;
