    INSERT INTO ielts_questions (group_id,question_number,stem,options,correct_answer) VALUES (v_grp,13,'What is the author''s main point in the final paragraph?','[{"letter":"A","text":"Sleep deprivation is largely harmless for most adults."},{"text":"Ambitious people naturally require less sleep.","letter":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"The cultural admiration of short sleep is mistaken and harmful."},{"letter":"D","text":"Heart disease is the leading consequence of poor sleep."}]'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb);
    INSERT INTO ielts_tests (slug,name,is_published,description) VALUES ('gri-reading-9','Gri Reading 9',true,'IELTS Academic Reading — The Stone That Heals Itself: The Secret of Roman Concrete') RETURNING id INTO v_test;
    INSERT INTO ielts_sections (test_id,section,duration_minutes) VALUES (v_test,'reading',20) RETURNING id INTO v_sec;
    INSERT INTO ielts_parts (section_id,part_number,title,passage_html) VALUES (v_sec,1,'The Stone That Heals Itself: The Secret of Roman Concrete','<p>For nearly two thousand years, the harbours, aqueducts and domed temples built by the Romans have withstood the pounding of waves, the tremor of earthquakes and the slow erosion of time. The Pantheon in Rome, completed around AD 126, still carries the largest unreinforced concrete dome ever constructed. Marine piers that were cast into the Mediterranean during the reign of the emperors remain intact, while harbour walls poured from modern concrete often begin to crumble within decades. This striking contrast has led engineers and materials scientists to ask a simple question: what did Roman builders know that we have forgotten?</p><p>Part of the answer lies in a grey volcanic powder. The Romans mixed lime with ash drawn from deposits around the Bay of Naples, particularly near the town of Pozzuoli, and the material took its name — pozzolana — from that location. When this ash was combined with lime and water, a chemical reaction produced a durable binder that set even underwater. Roman engineers observed the effect long before they could explain it; the architect Vitruvius, writing in the first century BC, recommended the ash without any knowledge of the chemistry involved. The volcanic particles were rich in reactive silica and alumina, which bound with the lime to form stable compounds far tougher than lime alone.</p><p>The behaviour of Roman marine concrete is especially remarkable. Rather than being slowly destroyed by seawater, as steel-reinforced structures are, it appears to have grown stronger. Seawater percolating through tiny cracks dissolved components of the volcanic ash and prompted the growth of rare interlocking minerals, including a form of aluminous tobermorite and a zeolite called phillipsite. These crystals reinforced the material from within, effectively turning a potential weakness into a source of strength. The sea, in other words, became an ally rather than an enemy.</p><p>A more recent discovery has shed light on a second mechanism. For a long time, small white lumps scattered through Roman concrete were dismissed as evidence of careless mixing or poor-quality raw materials. In 2023, however, a research team argued that these lime clasts were in fact functional. The scientists proposed that the Romans used a technique called hot mixing, in which quicklime was combined directly with the volcanic ash and water at high temperatures, rather than being slaked into a paste beforehand. This process left brittle, lime-rich fragments distributed throughout the finished material.</p><p>The value of these fragments becomes clear when the concrete cracks. Because water tends to travel along any fracture, it soon reaches a lime clast, dissolves it and carries the calcium into the gap. There the calcium recrystallises, sealing the crack before it can widen. In laboratory tests, samples made with the hot-mixing method fully closed cracks within two weeks, whereas concrete produced without lime clasts remained broken. The Roman material, in effect, could repair itself without any human intervention — a property that modern engineers are eager to reproduce.</p><p>Why, then, does contemporary concrete fall short? Most modern construction relies on Portland cement, patented in the nineteenth century, which is prized because it hardens quickly and predictably. This speed suits the demands of large-scale building, but it comes at a cost. Portland cement produces a denser, more rigid material that lacks the self-healing chemistry of the Roman recipe, and structures built from it usually depend on internal steel bars for tensile strength. When seawater or road salt reaches that steel, the metal rusts and expands, splitting the surrounding concrete apart. The reinforcement that gives modern concrete its strength is thus also the seed of its decay.</p><p>There is an environmental dimension as well. Manufacturing Portland cement requires heating limestone to very high temperatures, a process that releases enormous quantities of carbon dioxide; cement production is responsible for a significant share of global emissions. A concrete that lasts for centuries and mends its own cracks would need to be replaced far less often, reducing both waste and pollution. For this reason, laboratories are now attempting to recreate the ancient formula using modern materials, hoping to combine Roman durability with the convenience of Portland cement.</p><p>The Romans left no detailed manual explaining their methods, and much of their knowledge was lost after the fall of the empire. Yet the buildings themselves have survived as a kind of message across the centuries, quietly demonstrating that longevity need not be sacrificed for speed.</p>') RETURNING id INTO v_part;
